WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:21.870
Cornelius Vanderbilt - America’s First Tycoon

00:00:21.870 --> 00:00:25.980
Cornelius Vanderbilt wasn’t exactly what
you would expect out of one of the richest

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:26.980
men in the world.

00:00:26.980 --> 00:00:31.090
He was brutish, ill-mannered, barely literate,
and swore like a sailor.

00:00:31.090 --> 00:00:35.780
But, perhaps, it was these same traits that
made him the definition of the American Dream

00:00:35.780 --> 00:00:39.190
with all the good and the bad that that entailed.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:42.489
He rose up from nothing; from less than nothing,
in fact.

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:46.090
His Dutch ancestors came to the New World
as indentured servants.

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:51.270
But through hard work and a keen eye towards
the future he amassed a fortune seldom seen

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:52.739
before that time.

00:00:52.739 --> 00:00:55.370
That being said, he certainly was no saint.

00:00:55.370 --> 00:01:00.489
Like all the other industrialists and speculators
of his time, Vanderbilt stepped on plenty

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:02.160
of toes to make his money.

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.639
He was ruthless in business and didn’t come
to be regarded as America’s first robber

00:01:06.639 --> 00:01:10.700
baron for nothing.

00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:14.100
Early Years

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:21.039
Cornelius Vanderbilt was born on May 27, 1794,
in Staten Island, New York City, to Cornelius

00:01:21.039 --> 00:01:23.119
Van Der Bilt Sr. and Phebe Hand.

00:01:23.119 --> 00:01:28.209
His ancestors had come to North America 150
years before he was born, back when the territory

00:01:28.209 --> 00:01:31.859
was still part of a Dutch colony called New
Netherland.

00:01:31.859 --> 00:01:37.110
His great great great grandfather was a man
named Jan Aertsen who immigrated to America

00:01:37.110 --> 00:01:38.770
as an indentured servant.

00:01:38.770 --> 00:01:44.729
By the time Cornelius was born, his family’s
station had improved, but not by much.

00:01:44.729 --> 00:01:48.930
His parents were farmers and, to supplement
their income, his father also operated a ferry

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.439
that transported people and goods across the
New York Harbor.

00:01:52.439 --> 00:01:55.759
Young Cornelius had a very strong work ethic
ever since he was a boy.

00:01:55.759 --> 00:01:59.549
He labored on the farm from an early age and,
when he was 11 years old, he dropped out of

00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:00.809
school to work full-time.

00:02:00.809 --> 00:02:05.569
Although, clearly, things would work out well
for him, his lack of a formal education would

00:02:05.569 --> 00:02:07.840
haunt Vanderbilt for the rest of his life.

00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:11.920
Even in his later years, he struggled to write
English fluently and he was in the habit of

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:13.700
spelling words out phonetically.

00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.690
It was part of the reason why he could never
find acceptance within the ranks of the wealthy

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:21.629
elite and was always regarded as a boorish
outsider.

00:02:21.629 --> 00:02:26.061
Cornelius Vanderbilt might be remembered today
as a railroad tycoon, but it wasn’t trains

00:02:26.061 --> 00:02:29.110
that made him his first fortune, but rather
ships.

00:02:29.110 --> 00:02:33.390
His road to success started when he began
helping his father with his ferry service.

00:02:33.390 --> 00:02:37.530
He learned the business and, by the time he
was 16, Vanderbilt was ready to branch out

00:02:37.530 --> 00:02:38.530
on his own.

00:02:38.530 --> 00:02:43.219
He borrowed $100 from his parents to buy his
own ship, a two-masted sailing vessel called

00:02:43.219 --> 00:02:47.319
a periauger, and he paid them back by splitting
the profits with them.

00:02:47.319 --> 00:02:51.260
Vanderbilt faced stiff competition, but his
strategy was to undercut everyone else by

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:55.450
offering cheap fares for transport between
Staten Island and Manhattan.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:03:00.269
That being said, he was also an extremely
hard worker and constantly reinvested in his

00:03:00.269 --> 00:03:02.909
business by acquiring more ships.

00:03:02.909 --> 00:03:07.579
Vanderbilt quickly built up a reputation as
a reliable and assiduous ferryman.

00:03:07.579 --> 00:03:11.420
In fact, even though he was still just a young
man, he earned the nickname “The Commodore,”

00:03:11.420 --> 00:03:15.050
a moniker which he eagerly embraced and used
for the rest of his life.

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:19.950
Thanks to his good standing, Vanderbilt earned
an important government contract in 1812 when

00:03:19.950 --> 00:03:25.280
war broke out between the United States and
the United Kingdom to supply the forts and

00:03:25.280 --> 00:03:27.670
ferry soldiers around New York Harbor.

00:03:27.670 --> 00:03:31.860
This period of conflict proved quite prosperous
for Cornelius Vanderbilt.

00:03:31.860 --> 00:03:37.549
On the personal front, in 1813 he also married
his first wife, a cousin named Sophia Johnson.

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:41.920
They would go on to have 13 children together,
although they did not have a happy marriage.

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:46.049
By all accounts, Vanderbilt was not a very
caring father or husband.

00:03:46.049 --> 00:03:50.830
It had often been rumored that he regularly
cheated on Sophia with prostitutes and even

00:03:50.830 --> 00:03:53.969
had her committed to an insane asylum once.

00:03:53.969 --> 00:03:58.799
Even though he was steadily amassing a small
fortune as he was building up his fleet, in

00:03:58.799 --> 00:04:04.349
1817 Vanderbilt was offered a position he
couldn’t refuse - that of steamboat captain.

00:04:04.349 --> 00:04:08.760
For years, the Commodore had developed an
interest in steam-powered ships as he considered

00:04:08.760 --> 00:04:11.760
them superior to sailing vessels and the way
of the future.

00:04:11.760 --> 00:04:17.509
Moreover, the offer came from Thomas Gibbons,
former Mayor of Savannah, Georgia, and one

00:04:17.509 --> 00:04:20.019
of the wealthiest merchants around.

00:04:20.019 --> 00:04:24.000
Vanderbilt assumed command of the steamboat
Mouse and started ferrying people between

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.150
New York and New Jersey while, simultaneously,
maintaining control of his growing business.

00:04:29.150 --> 00:04:34.199
However, he soon discovered that the steamboat
trade in New York was run by a monopoly granted

00:04:34.199 --> 00:04:39.669
by the New York State Legislature to two men:
Robert Livingston, an influential New York

00:04:39.669 --> 00:04:44.919
politician and one of the Founding Fathers;
and Robert Fulton, the man credited with inventing

00:04:44.919 --> 00:04:47.140
the first commercially-viable steamboat.

00:04:47.140 --> 00:04:53.110
They were both dead by 1817, but their heirs
still had a vise-grip on the monopoly and

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:58.180
they had granted a license to run steamboats
on the Hudson River to former New Jersey senator

00:04:58.180 --> 00:05:00.160
and governor Aaron Ogden.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:04.750
This whole thing started from a personal rivalry
between Gibbons and Ogden.

00:05:04.750 --> 00:05:09.080
At first, the former tried to bankrupt the
latter by undercutting his prices.

00:05:09.080 --> 00:05:13.690
Eventually, Ogden sued and the matter went
to court where it actually resulted in a very

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:15.500
important, landmark ruling.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:20.430
In the case of Gibbons v. Ogden (1824), the
Supreme Court concluded that the commerce

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:24.639
clause in the Constitution which granted the
federal government the power to decide how

00:05:24.639 --> 00:05:29.139
interstate commerce was conducted also extended
to waterway navigation.

00:05:29.139 --> 00:05:33.889
Therefore, the state of New York had no right
to grant a monopoly in the first place, and

00:05:33.889 --> 00:05:36.210
the court ruled in favor of Gibbons.

00:05:36.210 --> 00:05:40.770
To support his employer, Vanderbilt had his
own case against Ogden, even hiring Daniel

00:05:40.770 --> 00:05:44.860
Webster, a noted statesman, to argue his case
before the Supreme Court.

00:05:44.860 --> 00:05:49.960
However, it was never heard as it was unnecessary
after the ruling in the previous case.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:55.050
Even so, Vanderbilt deserves no credit as
some sort of pioneering trustbuster.

00:05:55.050 --> 00:05:58.120
He only fought the monopoly because it worked
against him.

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:02.659
In the future, he would have no problem using
them to his advantage.

00:06:02.659 --> 00:06:07.479
The Birth of an Empire

00:06:07.479 --> 00:06:13.690
In 1826, Thomas Gibbons died, leaving his
business in the hands of his son, William.

00:06:13.690 --> 00:06:18.389
Cornelius Vanderbilt considered the wealthy
merchant as somewhat of a mentor to him, but

00:06:18.389 --> 00:06:21.190
he didn’t like William as he considered
him weak.

00:06:21.190 --> 00:06:25.569
He stayed on for a few more years until he
got his finances in order, but Vanderbilt

00:06:25.569 --> 00:06:29.280
left the business in 1828 and broke off on
his own again.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:35.450
He even bought his own steamboat - a 145-ton,
106-ft sidewheeler called the Citizen.

00:06:35.450 --> 00:06:38.650
This vessel was quickly followed by others
in the next few years.

00:06:38.650 --> 00:06:43.330
As we previously mentioned, Vanderbilt regularly
took the profits he made and reinvested back

00:06:43.330 --> 00:06:47.830
into his business, steadily building a fleet
of steamboats and opening new routes for his

00:06:47.830 --> 00:06:52.849
ferries such as the Long Island Sound, Albany,
Connecticut, and Providence.

00:06:52.849 --> 00:06:57.349
Of course, he faced competition in all the
new areas he expanded to, but these companies

00:06:57.349 --> 00:07:02.110
usually found out quickly that it was dangerous
to go against the Commodore.

00:07:02.110 --> 00:07:05.639
He was ruthless when it came to business.

00:07:05.639 --> 00:07:10.330
Because he had a wealth of resources, his
go-to strategy involved undercutting his opponents’

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:14.669
prices, oftentimes to the point where they
would either go bankrupt or be forced to sell

00:07:14.669 --> 00:07:15.669
to him.

00:07:15.669 --> 00:07:19.479
In the cases of some companies that also had
deep pockets, they would often pay Vanderbilt

00:07:19.479 --> 00:07:24.500
large sums of money to leave the area and
take his ferries on another route.

00:07:24.500 --> 00:07:28.849
One notable example was when the Commodore
wanted to put his ships on the Hudson River,

00:07:28.849 --> 00:07:30.860
traveling between New York City and Albany.

00:07:30.860 --> 00:07:35.189
This route was dominated by a monopoly called
the Hudson River Steamboat Association, but

00:07:35.189 --> 00:07:37.659
Vanderbilt only saw it as a challenge.

00:07:37.659 --> 00:07:41.610
This was at a time when Andrew Jackson was
running a populist presidential campaign based

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.039
around “the common man.”

00:07:43.039 --> 00:07:48.099
He had gained popularity by portraying himself
as a man of the people, fighting the corrupt

00:07:48.099 --> 00:07:49.469
aristocracy.

00:07:49.469 --> 00:07:50.570
Vanderbilt did the same thing.

00:07:50.570 --> 00:07:54.550
It wasn’t hard, after all, he did come from
a working class family and his behavior and

00:07:54.550 --> 00:07:58.689
demeanor were certainly more at home in a
bar than a banquet.

00:07:58.689 --> 00:08:02.750
He dubbed his ferry service the “People’s
Line” and offered cheap rates that working

00:08:02.750 --> 00:08:04.020
men could afford.

00:08:04.020 --> 00:08:08.780
Unsurprisingly, this strategy worked really
well and, soon enough, the Steamboat Association

00:08:08.780 --> 00:08:15.680
offered him $100,000 to leave the Hudson River,
plus another $5,000 a year to stay away.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:19.680
Going into Nicaragua

00:08:19.680 --> 00:08:26.520
For years, Vanderbilt focused his business
mainly in the region between Boston and New

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:32.700
York, but he organized a major expansion in
1849 prompted by the California Gold Rush.

00:08:32.700 --> 00:08:37.690
He knew that hundreds of thousands, even millions
of people would be traveling that way, many

00:08:37.690 --> 00:08:42.520
of them looking to settle permanently in the
unorganized California territory which, at

00:08:42.520 --> 00:08:44.910
that point, was still lobbying for statehood.

00:08:44.910 --> 00:08:49.350
When California finally became a state in
1850, demand for transportation between the

00:08:49.350 --> 00:08:54.500
East and West Coasts skyrocketed and, all
of a sudden, a new multi-million dollar venture

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:55.820
was born.

00:08:55.820 --> 00:09:00.170
Unsurprisingly, Vanderbilt wanted a piece
of that pie and started sending steamships

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:01.300
along this route.

00:09:01.300 --> 00:09:06.250
Back then, the only way to travel from New
York to San Francisco by water was to round

00:09:06.250 --> 00:09:09.460
Cape Horn at the southerly tip of South America.

00:09:09.460 --> 00:09:13.250
However, this trip was long and time meant
money.

00:09:13.250 --> 00:09:17.970
It was still quicker and less dangerous than
traveling overland across the United States,

00:09:17.970 --> 00:09:22.040
but Vanderbilt sought a way to speed up the
journey even more.

00:09:22.040 --> 00:09:24.920
The solution was a canal across Nicaragua.

00:09:24.920 --> 00:09:28.850
It would have shaved days off each trip.

00:09:28.850 --> 00:09:33.350
Vanderbilt even founded the Accessory Transit
Company or ATC whose purpose was to secure

00:09:33.350 --> 00:09:36.120
transportation across the Central American
country.

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:41.560
However, for various political and economical
reasons, the project never went into construction

00:09:41.560 --> 00:09:46.690
and, eventually, it was established that a
canal across Panama would be more efficient.

00:09:46.690 --> 00:09:49.260
That being said, the ATC was not a failed
venture.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:51.150
Quite the opposite, in fact.

00:09:51.150 --> 00:09:54.390
Vanderbilt may not have been able to secure
a route that took passengers from the East

00:09:54.390 --> 00:09:58.900
Coast to the West Coast on a single ship,
but he developed a course that was a mix of

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:00.790
land and water travel.

00:10:00.790 --> 00:10:04.520
His customers would board a steamboat in New
York which took them to the eastern coast

00:10:04.520 --> 00:10:05.860
of Nicaragua.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:09.870
From there, the goods and passengers were
loaded onto smaller vessels capable of going

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:13.270
up the San Juan River and crossing Lake Nicaragua.

00:10:13.270 --> 00:10:17.670
When they reached land, they traveled by stagecoach
to the western coast where they boarded a

00:10:17.670 --> 00:10:20.390
different steamer headed for San Francisco.

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:25.470
It was the fastest and cheapest way of reaching
the East Coast and it made Vanderbilt a nice

00:10:25.470 --> 00:10:29.650
bundle of money, but he didn’t get everything
he aimed for.

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:35.280
What the Commodore wanted was a big, fat government
contract to also transport mail, but it went

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:39.710
instead to some of his competitors who founded
the Pacific Mail Steamship Company and the

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:42.220
United States Mail Steamship Company.

00:10:42.220 --> 00:10:46.670
Even so, the Commodore still had an ace up
his sleeve as his opponents used a route across

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:47.670
Panama.

00:10:47.670 --> 00:10:52.030
It was similar, but longer, so Vanderbilt
went back to his tried-and-true method of

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:56.280
slashing his prices to steal all of his competition’s
clientele.

00:10:56.280 --> 00:11:01.190
It worked, but when Gold Rush fever started
to die down, the Commodore saw no reason to

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:06.610
keep this route so he accepted a large monthly
stipend from his opponents to close down the

00:11:06.610 --> 00:11:18.920
ATC.

00:11:18.920 --> 00:12:20.500
The War of the Commodores

00:12:20.500 --> 00:12:48.680
While Vanderbilt was expanding his operations
to the West Coast, he also started offering

00:12:48.680 --> 00:12:50.600
transatlantic journeys to Europe.

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:55.580
Again, this venture proved profitable, but
the Commodore also learned that not even he

00:12:55.580 --> 00:12:56.580
was infallible.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:13:01.580
As before, his new route had competition - the
British Cunard Steamship Line, among others.

00:13:01.580 --> 00:13:04.620
Vanderbilt tried to squash it like he always
did, but failed.

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:08.570
The British government was keen to see the
company succeed against American and German

00:13:08.570 --> 00:13:13.950
firms so they provided a low-interest loan
plus a generous annual subsidy to ensure that

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:19.180
the Cunard Line had the capital to both build
top-of-the-line ships and to compete with

00:13:19.180 --> 00:13:21.580
Vanderbilt’s undercutting tactics.

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:26.810
Another unexpected setback occurred in 1853
when Vanderbilt saw fit, for the first time

00:13:26.810 --> 00:13:28.680
ever, to take a vacation.

00:13:28.680 --> 00:13:33.000
He was almost 60 years old and had been working
for most of his life so the Commodore decided

00:13:33.000 --> 00:13:36.480
that, if he were to go on holiday, he would
do it in style.

00:13:36.480 --> 00:13:40.990
Vanderbilt bought a giant, luxurious yacht
he dubbed the North Star and took his whole

00:13:40.990 --> 00:13:45.360
family on a grand tour of Europe, at a total
cost of half a million dollars.

00:13:45.360 --> 00:13:50.120
Whilst he was vacationing, the Commodore left
the management of ATC in Nicaragua in the

00:13:50.120 --> 00:13:54.170
hands of two men, Charles Morgan and Cornelius
Garrison.

00:13:54.170 --> 00:13:58.570
In came another American named William Walker
who was a filibuster which, back then, meant

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:03.300
that he was a sort of insurgent who would
rally troops to invade parts of Latin America,

00:14:03.300 --> 00:14:06.560
take over by force and set up English-speaking
colonies.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:11.540
There were a few filibusters during the 19th
century as Manifest Destiny was still a big

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:16.080
thing, but Walker was probably the most famous
of them all because he actually succeeded

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:17.970
twice...sort of.

00:14:17.970 --> 00:14:23.190
First, in 1853, Walker and dozens of his followers
invaded Mexico and actually conquered the

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:28.430
Baja California Territory by assaulting its
capital of La Paz and capturing the governor.

00:14:28.430 --> 00:14:32.430
He then declared it the Republic of Lower
California, a sovereign state with him as

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:33.430
president.

00:14:33.430 --> 00:14:34.980
Of course, it didn’t last long.

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:39.010
As Walker and his men ran out of supplies,
desertions became frequent and he also had

00:14:39.010 --> 00:14:42.880
to relocate his headquarters several times
because the Mexican army was just a little

00:14:42.880 --> 00:14:44.010
upset with him.

00:14:44.010 --> 00:14:47.950
In the end, he had no choice but to cross
the border and surrender to an American army.

00:14:47.950 --> 00:14:52.240
He was arrested and charged with an illegal
war, but was actually acquitted after giving

00:14:52.240 --> 00:14:55.230
an impassioned speech that swayed the jury.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:59.580
All Walker learned from this experience was
that he needed better financing.

00:14:59.580 --> 00:15:03.180
In 1855, he was ready to try again, this time
in Nicaragua.

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:08.130
While the Commodore was still on his extended
vacation, Walker approached Morgan and Garrison

00:15:08.130 --> 00:15:11.770
and, in exchange for their support of his
coup, he promised that, once he was the new

00:15:11.770 --> 00:15:16.760
president, he would revoke ATC’s charter
and issue a new own in their name.

00:15:16.760 --> 00:15:18.410
And that’s exactly what happened.

00:15:18.410 --> 00:15:23.620
In 1856, William Walker became the new President
of the Republic of Nicaragua and a new transit

00:15:23.620 --> 00:15:29.120
business owned by Morgan and Garrison had
exclusive rights to operate in the country.

00:15:29.120 --> 00:15:32.970
Understandably, Vanderbilt was furious when
he learned of their treachery.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:37.140
According to an apocryphal story, he wrote
them a short and ominous letter where he said

00:15:37.140 --> 00:15:39.880
"Gentlemen: You have undertaken to cheat me.

00:15:39.880 --> 00:15:42.990
I won't sue you, for the law is too slow.

00:15:42.990 --> 00:15:44.470
I'll ruin you.

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:47.240
Yours truly, Cornelius Vanderbilt."

00:15:47.240 --> 00:15:49.480
And that is, pretty much, what he did.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:54.310
He set up another transit company that passed
through Panama with dirt cheap prices to steal

00:15:54.310 --> 00:15:55.370
their customers.

00:15:55.370 --> 00:16:00.350
He refused to take any passengers to or from
Nicaragua and convinced the nearby Central

00:16:00.350 --> 00:16:03.650
American countries to not recognize Walker’s
government.

00:16:03.650 --> 00:16:07.890
Back in the United States, he sued Morgan
and Garrison in a legal battle the American

00:16:07.890 --> 00:16:10.370
media dubbed “The War of the Commodores.”

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:15.400
And, most decisively, he sent agents into
Nicaragua to cause chaos and convince the

00:16:15.400 --> 00:16:17.540
locals to rebel against Walker.

00:16:17.540 --> 00:16:22.450
It worked and, in 1857, the filibuster surrendered
to the U.S. army again.

00:16:22.450 --> 00:16:26.230
Meanwhile, Morgan and Garrison’s business
went bankrupt and Vanderbilt assumed control

00:16:26.230 --> 00:16:28.860
over the region’s transit system again.

00:16:28.860 --> 00:16:32.981
As far as Walker was concerned, he once again
was acquitted as many Americans regarded him

00:16:32.981 --> 00:16:33.981
as a hero.

00:16:33.981 --> 00:16:38.830
Then he went and tried the same thing in Honduras,
but things went a little different that time.

00:16:38.830 --> 00:16:45.210
The Honduran authorities captured him and,
in 1860, executed him by firing squad.

00:16:45.210 --> 00:16:50.230
From Ships to Trains

00:16:50.230 --> 00:16:55.450
The 1860s were a troubling time for Cornelius
Vanderbilt, as they were for most Americans,

00:16:55.450 --> 00:16:56.670
because of the Civil War.

00:16:56.670 --> 00:16:58.730
On a personal level, he suffered losses.

00:16:58.730 --> 00:17:03.060
His youngest and favorite son, George Washington
Vanderbilt, died in the war after contracting

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:04.060
malaria.

00:17:04.060 --> 00:17:08.309
An inveterate misogynist, the Commodore never
showed much affection to his daughters and

00:17:08.309 --> 00:17:11.569
he considered his other two sons to be disappointments.

00:17:11.569 --> 00:17:16.100
One of them, Cornelius Jeremiah, was a degenerate
gambler who was on bad terms with his father

00:17:16.100 --> 00:17:19.130
after the elder Cornelius locked him up in
a lunatic asylum.

00:17:19.130 --> 00:17:24.079
The other one, William Henry, the Commodore
simply regarded as weak and good for nothing.

00:17:24.079 --> 00:17:28.169
Eventually, Vanderbilt changed his tune about
William after he took a poor farm in Staten

00:17:28.169 --> 00:17:30.159
Island and made it profitable.

00:17:30.159 --> 00:17:34.250
Since then, Vanderbilt integrated his son
more and more into his business and, eventually,

00:17:34.250 --> 00:17:37.389
it was William who inherited most of his father’s
estate.

00:17:37.389 --> 00:17:39.919
In 1868, Vanderbilt’s wife, Sophia, died.

00:17:39.919 --> 00:17:44.830
After her passing, the Commodore began visiting
mediums, particularly the Claflin sisters.

00:17:44.830 --> 00:17:49.309
He fell in love with the younger sibling,
Tennessee Claflin, and, according to some

00:17:49.309 --> 00:17:51.480
sources, even proposed marriage.

00:17:51.480 --> 00:17:55.210
This worried his family who were concerned
that Vanderbilt might leave his fortune to

00:17:55.210 --> 00:18:00.190
a stranger so they played Cupid and fixed
him up with a distant cousin who was four

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:01.700
decades younger than him.

00:18:01.700 --> 00:18:04.039
This cousin had an unusual name for a woman.

00:18:04.039 --> 00:18:05.480
She was called Frank Armstrong.

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:09.720
We’re not exactly sure why, but at least
one historian asserts that this was due to

00:18:09.720 --> 00:18:15.110
a misguided promise from her parents to a
close friend to name their first child Frank,

00:18:15.110 --> 00:18:16.629
even if it wasn’t a boy.

00:18:16.629 --> 00:18:20.759
Anyway, Cornelius and Frank eloped in Canada
in 1869.

00:18:20.759 --> 00:18:24.970
As far as the Claflin sisters were concerned,
they soon gave up their spiritualist ways

00:18:24.970 --> 00:18:27.169
and became noted suffragists.

00:18:27.169 --> 00:18:30.799
With Vanderbilt’s backing, they also got
into the business world and became the first

00:18:30.799 --> 00:18:33.200
women to open a brokerage firm on Wall Street.

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:37.649
The older sister dropped the “Claflin”
from her name and became known as Victoria

00:18:37.649 --> 00:18:41.929
Woodhull, the first ever female candidate
for the Presidency of the United States.

00:18:41.929 --> 00:18:48.000
Going back to the Civil War, this period represented
a huge shift in Vanderbilt’s business practices.

00:18:48.000 --> 00:18:52.679
No longer impressed with the capabilities
of steamboats, he now looked to the railroad

00:18:52.679 --> 00:18:53.679
as the future.

00:18:53.679 --> 00:18:57.539
Truth be told, the Commodore had shown interest
in trains for decades.

00:18:57.539 --> 00:19:02.000
He might have invested in them even sooner
if not for his involvement in one of the first

00:19:02.000 --> 00:19:04.330
fatal train accidents in American history.

00:19:04.330 --> 00:19:09.429
All the way back in 1833, the Hightstown Rail
Accident occurred in New Jersey when a carriage

00:19:09.429 --> 00:19:13.080
caught fire, caused an axle to break and derailed
the train.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:15.779
One person was killed and dozens were injured.

00:19:15.779 --> 00:19:19.779
Among them was Cornelius Vanderbilt who broke
his leg in the crash.

00:19:19.779 --> 00:19:23.740
In the decades following that event, the Commodore
only had minor dealings with the rail industry.

00:19:23.740 --> 00:19:28.630
He occasionally bought shares in new railroads,
but most of his efforts were concentrated

00:19:28.630 --> 00:19:29.630
in Nicaragua.

00:19:29.630 --> 00:19:33.779
Eventually, though, he could no longer ignore
the potential for huge profits offered by

00:19:33.779 --> 00:19:35.929
this new mode of transportation.

00:19:35.929 --> 00:19:39.440
Starting with the late 1850s, he began selling
off his fleet of ships.

00:19:39.440 --> 00:19:44.000
During the Civil War, he even donated his
most prized vessel, the appropriately-named

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:48.360
Vanderbilt, to the Union Navy to use against
the Confederates who dominated the seas with

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:50.460
the help of their ironclads.

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:54.789
Vanderbilt even acted as advisor when it came
to all things maritime, but he refused an

00:19:54.789 --> 00:19:59.400
official position in Lincoln’s cabinet as
he wanted to avoid politics at any cost.

00:19:59.400 --> 00:20:02.529
The Commodore wasn’t very interested in
building railroads.

00:20:02.529 --> 00:20:06.259
He preferred to buy ones that already existed
and form a monopoly.

00:20:06.259 --> 00:20:09.210
His first major purchase was the New York
and Harlem Railroad.

00:20:09.210 --> 00:20:13.610
At the time, it wasn’t considered particularly
valuable as it only ran trains for part of

00:20:13.610 --> 00:20:15.919
the journey before connecting to horse-drawn
carriages.

00:20:15.919 --> 00:20:21.570
However, it had prime position as the only
railway that entered downtown Manhattan.

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:25.580
Vanderbilt brought in his son, William, as
vice-president of the Harlem line and, together,

00:20:25.580 --> 00:20:27.500
they soon made it profitable.

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:31.610
Next up, he took control of the Hudson River
Railroad in 1864 and the New York Central

00:20:31.610 --> 00:20:33.570
Railroad in 1867.

00:20:33.570 --> 00:20:38.970
He also bought smaller, adjacent lines to
consolidate them all into one giant railroad

00:20:38.970 --> 00:20:39.970
empire.

00:20:39.970 --> 00:20:43.000
Vanderbilt was already 70 years old when he
decided to get into railroads.

00:20:43.000 --> 00:20:47.990
He took a backseat role and mostly delegated
orders to William and other subordinates,

00:20:47.990 --> 00:20:51.830
although he did enjoy taking inspection trips
to oversee his domains.

00:20:51.830 --> 00:20:55.539
It seemed that his ruthlessness had subsided
somewhat with age.

00:20:55.539 --> 00:20:58.840
Very rarely were there any cutthroat tactics
to get his way.

00:20:58.840 --> 00:21:02.580
More often than not, Vanderbilt preferred
simply to buy enough stock in a railroad until

00:21:02.580 --> 00:21:05.980
he became the majority shareholder and then
appointed himself president.

00:21:05.980 --> 00:21:10.529
He even dabbled in philanthropy, donating
$1 million to establish the Vanderbilt University

00:21:10.529 --> 00:21:12.490
in Nashville, Tennessee.

00:21:12.490 --> 00:21:17.870
The last major addition to the Vanderbilt
empire occurred in 1876 when the tycoon acquired

00:21:17.870 --> 00:21:19.220
the Canada Southern Railway.

00:21:19.220 --> 00:21:23.049
Soon after that, the 82-year-old Commodore
fell gravely ill.

00:21:23.049 --> 00:21:29.009
His death was slow and painful, being bedridden
for months before passing away on January

00:21:29.009 --> 00:21:30.919
4, 1877.

00:21:30.919 --> 00:21:34.929
He remained a cantankerous, grumpy malcontent
until the very end.

00:21:34.929 --> 00:21:39.330
On his better days, he would curse out his
physicians and throw water bottles at them.

00:21:39.330 --> 00:21:44.110
On one occasion, he had to be physically restrained
after trying to attack the media men who were

00:21:44.110 --> 00:21:47.570
keeping watch outside of his house to report
on his death.

00:21:47.570 --> 00:21:51.409
Vanderbilt’s death was followed by a long
and messy lawsuit over his assets.

00:21:51.409 --> 00:21:56.820
Not wanting to split up his fortune, he left
around $90 million of his $105 million estate

00:21:56.820 --> 00:22:00.840
to his son, William, while Frank and the rest
of his children received much smaller amounts

00:22:00.840 --> 00:22:01.879
in cash or stock.

00:22:01.879 --> 00:22:29.809
And, in case you were wondering, that fortune
means well over $200 billion in modern currency.

